WEBVTT

00:00:00.644 --> 00:00:19.668
<v Casey>Morning, good to have you. Monday's Film and Screen stretch, so let's get into it. Today we've got the Quesnel mayor vowing to tackle public safety in her first hundred days, plus a B.C. tribunal that overturned X's $100,000 penalty. And the Deep Dive asks who decides what gets painted on a downtown Williams Lake wall. Stay with us.

00:00:33.143 --> 00:00:52.471
<v Casey>Welcome to Cariboo Signals, an AI-generated review of the latest tech news and ideas that impact our rural communities. I'm Casey. It's Monday, November 16th, 2026. Today's theme is Arts, Culture &amp; Digital Storytelling. Here in the Cariboo, on the traditional and unceded territories of the Secwépemc, Tŝilhqot'in, and Dakelh nations.

00:00:53.471 --> 00:01:01.407
<v Riley>And I'm Riley. We're looking at who decides what gets seen, from newsrooms and streaming platforms to a fresh mural on a downtown wall.

00:01:02.149 --> 00:01:26.749
<v Casey>Out at Horsefly Lake this morning it's overcast and around 1 degree, with a high of 6 and a low of 0 today, slight snow showers through the day and about a one-in-three chance of precipitation. Across the Cariboo, 100 Mile House is around minus 1 and partly cloudy, Williams Lake is around 1 and overcast, Quesnel is around 2 with slight snow showers, and Redstone is around minus 2 and partly cloudy. On to the news.

00:01:28.149 --> 00:01:36.861
<v Riley>Mostly civic at the top today, with new council seats and an old memorial, then a wider look at who makes, runs and polices the stories we all see.

00:01:38.456 --> 00:02:03.160
<v Casey>Williams Lake has honoured its war dead in public for decades. The Williams Lake and District Cenotaph went up in 1968, a joint project of the municipality and Royal Canadian Legion Branch 139, and the Tribune covered the flag-raising that year. Sixteen years later the cenotaph was moved beside city hall and rededicated on November 3rd, 1984, and a Korean War plaque was dedicated in 1994.

00:02:03.610 --> 00:02:06.754
<v Riley>Moved once in sixteen years, and people kept marching to it.

00:02:08.544 --> 00:02:31.960
<v Riley>Quesnel has a new council. Coralee Oakes took the oath of office as mayor on November 4th, sworn in by Judge Jonathan Hutchison along with six councillors. Four are returning: Tony Goulet, Mitch Vik, Laurey-Anne Roodenburg and Scott Elliott. Two are newcomers: Jeremy Drew and Ryan Adams. The headline has the new mayor vowing to tackle public safety over her first 100 days.

00:02:32.410 --> 00:02:37.058
<v Casey>A hundred days is a short clock for public safety. We'll see what gets counted by then.

00:02:38.816 --> 00:03:00.440
<v Casey>Entertainment 360 is buying Brigade, a small talent firm. Brigade represents Substack writers Leandra Medine Cohen and Emily Sundberg, podcast hosts, and fashion creators, and its founder Max Stein will join Entertainment 360 as a partner. The same company already represents film stars such as Margot Robbie and Julianne Moore. Neither firm disclosed the price.

00:03:01.943 --> 00:03:24.511
<v Riley>OrbitalWiki has a feature that turns satellite passes into music. It tracks nearly 17,000 satellites, and each time one reaches its highest point in the sky above you, it plays a note. The instrument and octave come from that satellite's orbit, so no two passes sound quite the same, and you can speed up the timescale if you want something livelier.

00:03:24.961 --> 00:03:28.673
<v Casey>Seventeen thousand objects overhead, and it sounds like a lullaby.

00:03:30.360 --> 00:04:01.878
<v Casey>The Mississippi Free Press is a Jackson-based nonprofit, and its news editor Ashton Pittman says the paper has tracked the details of the state's county election system for years. This year its reporters found eighteen incorrect polling locations in state voter resources. A U.S. Senate race between Cindy Hyde-Smith and Scott Colom came down to a slim margin, and Pittman and six reporters were still tracking ballot counts more than a week after polls closed. In that reporting the race was too close to call, and the outcome hinges on affidavit ballots.

00:04:02.328 --> 00:04:07.432
<v Riley>Seven people still counting a week later. Local election coverage is expensive labour.

00:04:09.256 --> 00:04:30.240
<v Riley>A new study finds journalism school faculty act as hidden infrastructure for local news. Professors put student work in front of real readers, and the study flags burnout as a risk for the people doing that extra work. The Nieman Lab writer's point is that passion alone isn't enough, since a nonprofit still needs revenue to keep its doors open.

00:04:30.690 --> 00:04:34.418
<v Casey>So local news runs partly on professors' spare hours. Who budgets for that?

00:04:36.078 --> 00:05:00.950
<v Casey>A new Royal Academy exhibition in London looks back at Guggenheim Jeune, the gallery Peggy Guggenheim ran there. It was short-lived, but it staged more than twenty shows, including work by Vasily Kandinsky and Jean Cocteau, and it shook up a conservative British art scene. The article also describes a 1938 show that was held up at customs, with pieces by Constantin Brâncuși and Alexander Calder among the works stuck at the border.

00:05:01.400 --> 00:05:06.128
<v Riley>A show stuck at customs in 1938. Distribution problems are older than streaming.

00:05:07.927 --> 00:05:43.549
<v Riley>The B.C. Civil Resolution Tribunal has overturned a $100,000 penalty against X. The penalty, imposed in September 2025, was for failing to delete an intimate image posted without a woman's consent. The tribunal ruled that blocking access to the image in Canada was a reasonable step under provincial law. The image stayed available outside Canada, and the tribunal accepted X's argument that a worldwide takedown could interfere with other countries' legal systems. Outgoing Attorney General Niki Sharma had criticized X's refusal to remove the image.

00:05:43.999 --> 00:05:47.391
<v Casey>Blocked in Canada, still up everywhere else. That isn't deleted.

00:05:49.226 --> 00:06:22.576
<v Casey>Meta, Google and TikTok have each sued California Attorney General Rob Bonta to block AB 1709. Governor Gavin Newsom signed the California law in September, and it's slated to take effect at the start of the new year. It bars autoplay videos and personalized feeds for users under 16, and platforms must delete those users' accounts if they don't switch the features off. Fines can reach $50,000 per affected user. Meta argues the law violates the U.S. First Amendment and that Section 230 preempts it, and TikTok and Google made similar arguments.

00:06:23.987 --> 00:06:46.867
<v Riley>The Public Domain Review profiles Urs Graf the Elder, a Swiss Renaissance artist who was also a mercenary. Writer Miya Tokumitsu describes his pen drawings of soldiers and sex workers, full of violence and a kind of melancholy, and says his imagery reflects Swiss martial culture of the period. Graf lived the violence he drew, which is part of why the drawings read so differently.

00:06:48.445 --> 00:07:06.357
<v Casey>The Tyee's headline says the Vancouver Aquatic Centre demolition is back on. Residents fought to save the 50-metre pool in the West End, and they lost. Separately, The Tyee says roughly half its budget now comes from more than 10,000 paying readers, and it has more than doubled its journalism team over the past decade.

00:07:06.807 --> 00:07:11.287
<v Riley>Ten thousand paying members is a lot of people choosing what gets written.

00:07:12.935 --> 00:07:42.269
<v Riley>After social media speculation that the horror novel Shy Girl was largely AI-generated, Hachette cancelled its upcoming U.S. publication and dropped the book in the UK, where it had already been released. Now authors face the reverse problem: proving their manuscripts are their own. WIRED asks whether paper-trail tools can do that. Andrea Bartz, the lead plaintiff in a class action against Anthropic, is also part of this story. For the record, this show's scripts come entirely from Anthropic's Claude.

00:07:42.719 --> 00:07:45.735
<v Casey>Proving you wrote your own book is a cost nobody budgets for.

00:07:47.431 --> 00:08:07.087
<v Casey>A Northwestern University Local News Initiative report looks at what local outlets lose when their staff stops working under one roof. Published November 10th, it follows outlets that downsized offices after the pandemic. Columbia College Chicago professor Jackie Spinner named the outlets that gave up or reduced physical space for mostly remote work.

00:08:07.537 --> 00:08:11.769
<v Riley>Office rent shows up on a spreadsheet. The hallway conversations don't.

00:08:13.462 --> 00:08:43.006
<v Riley>New York has begun a $184 million reconstruction of 70 Mulberry Street in Chinatown, a building badly damaged by a 2020 fire. Work started on Monday, November 9th, and completion is expected in 2030. When it's finished, the building will house or make room for the five community organizations that were there before, including the Museum of Chinese in America. A permanent three-part installation by artist Jennifer Wen Ma is part of the plan.

00:08:43.455 --> 00:08:47.367
<v Casey>Ten years from fire to finish. Tenants written back in, at least on paper.

00:08:48.967 --> 00:09:03.759
<v Casey>Poe reviewed the first chapters of Dickens's Barnaby Rudge for Graham's Magazine and called its talking raven, Grip, intensely amusing. He predicted how the novel would end, and when he reviewed the finished book he had been right. The two writers met the following year.

00:09:05.203 --> 00:09:29.187
<v Riley>Romare Bearden: A Life in Collage is a new documentary heading to DOC NYC. Instead of a straight biography, it jumps across Bearden's life to follow themes, including the trains that recur in his work. It draws on generous access to recordings of Bearden himself, speaking about his memories and process. The Hyperallergic reviewer says the best part is watching people interact with the work.

00:09:29.637 --> 00:09:32.509
<v Casey>Built on his own recordings. Somebody had to keep those.

00:09:34.215 --> 00:09:59.103
<v Casey>A survey for Epidemic Sound, a music and sound platform based in Sweden, found that 64 percent of six-figure creators and artists in the U.S. feel large or very large pressure to use AI. Among the lowest earners that figure is 19 percent. Sapio Research surveyed 2,000 professional creators and music artists between March and April, and the higher earners also reported using fully AI-generated content at higher rates.

00:09:59.553 --> 00:10:06.193
<v Riley>Under that kind of pressure, who signs the work and whose wall it goes on? We'll come back to walls shortly.

00:10:06.643 --> 00:10:09.339
<v Casey>First, a gallery that shows the region's painters.

00:10:10.739 --> 00:10:33.995
<v Riley>Station House Gallery is Williams Lake's primary visual arts gallery, and it shows work by artists from across the region. If you want to see what painters in this part of the Cariboo are making, that's the place to start. Look them up at stationhousegallery.com, then go stand in front of the work yourself. A painting on a screen and a painting on a wall are two different experiences.

00:10:34.634 --> 00:10:39.418
<v Casey>A room that shows local painters to their neighbours. That's the subject we're turning to next.

00:10:39.518 --> 00:10:42.214
<v Casey>Which brings us to two new walls on Third Avenue North.

00:10:43.614 --> 00:11:12.398
<v Riley>Two artists, two downtown Williams Lake walls, and each one took home $5,000 from the public art grant. The City of Williams Lake and the Downtown Williams Lake Business Improvement Association fund that program together, $5,000 apiece, for a $10,000 pool that can go to one project or be split. So a painter gets paid, gets a wall the whole street sees, and gets a public unveiling. For a first-time muralist, that's a strong start.

00:11:12.941 --> 00:11:25.533
<v Casey>Paid, yes. Who set the frame? Those are two very different walls. Courtney Patenaude's piece is an abstract design on a downtown restaurant wall. The restaurant owns that surface, so the owner still decides what goes up on it.

00:11:26.087 --> 00:11:41.039
<v Riley>Which makes the host a sort of landlord with a say. Jorgensen's situation is different. Her panel is the third in the Cariboo Reflections Mural Project, on the back wall of the Cariboo Regional District and library building at 180 3rd Ave. N.

00:11:41.707 --> 00:11:48.251
<v Casey>A public building with a name on the series. Three panels in, that's a frame. Who chose the first two, and who chooses the next?

00:11:48.513 --> 00:12:13.609
<v Riley>Dwayne Davis and Steven Gosling-Davis painted the first two in 2025. Davis's panel carries the daytime: a logging truck, cattle, cowboys, and a red pickup standing in for ranching and the Stampede. And the next panel already has a name on it. Jazmyn Lyons was selected to contribute one in 2026. So a numbered panel in a named project works like a door. A newcomer walks in through it.

00:12:14.207 --> 00:12:28.435
<v Casey>Walk in to what, though? The whole project is planned as six panels over 240 feet, tracing the Cariboo's daily cycle and its seasonal events. A painter joining that is choosing a place in someone else's sequence. Choosing a subject is a different act.

00:12:28.435 --> 00:12:39.139
<v Riley>Ha. Every mural in every town starts with somebody saying yes to a wall. Patenaude's wall had a yes behind it too. The real test is whether the yes limits what she can paint.

00:12:39.775 --> 00:12:44.111
<v Casey>For Jorgensen, the yes came with a landscape attached. Her panel is the Chilcotin.

00:12:44.628 --> 00:12:55.532
<v Riley>And she painted it beautifully, by the look of the Tribune photo. A cycle-of-the-day project tells you roughly where you sit. It still leaves the brushwork, the colour, the mood to the painter.

00:12:56.104 --> 00:13:04.112
<v Casey>Roughly where you sit is a brief. Whether anyone wrote it down for the painter or just handed it over in conversation, it shaped the wall before the first coat.

00:13:04.735 --> 00:13:24.359
<v Riley>Then compare it with the other wall. An abstract on a business wall is about as open as a commission gets. The grant also carries a geographic rule: the BIA half has to stay inside downtown boundaries, and the city half can go anywhere in town. Those are the conditions on the record, and they're about place, not subject.

00:13:24.922 --> 00:13:34.722
<v Casey>Abstract has another side. It's the easiest subject to approve, because nobody can point at a face or a name and object to it. The safety sits in the selection, and the art inherits it.

00:13:35.294 --> 00:13:45.486
<v Riley>Or it's a first-time painter picking a style she can finish on a deadline. Plenty of good public art starts that way. I'd rather judge the mural than the choice of style.

00:13:45.804 --> 00:13:49.748
<v Casey>Judge the mural, fine. I'm asking who judged it before the paint went up.

00:13:50.343 --> 00:14:01.775
<v Riley>The Chinatown rebuild we mentioned up top answers that question one way. Jennifer Wen Ma's installation was planned into the public project before the walls exist. The art is part of the build from the start.

00:14:02.303 --> 00:14:17.703
<v Casey>That's the model where the public body writes the brief into the budget. Here, the money is $10,000 across two artists from two sponsors, and the frame on the restaurant wall gets set by whoever owns the bricks. I'd rather have the frame written down first.

00:14:18.267 --> 00:14:31.195
<v Riley>Write it down, sure. Where I push back is the idea that a grant-funded mural can't be a real commission. Two paid commissions, two unveilings, two named sponsors on the record. It's imperfect, but it's checkable.

00:14:31.793 --> 00:14:36.785
<v Casey>Checkable only if someone publishes the rules. Otherwise it's a press photo and a sponsor logo.

00:14:36.884 --> 00:14:47.412
<v Casey>And paid is a loose word. Each artist received $5,000. A wall that size eats materials before the fee gets paid: paint, primer, maybe a lift or scaffolding, and the hours on top.

00:14:48.075 --> 00:14:58.203
<v Riley>I'd want to know that split too. Still, a $5,000 cheque beats an unpaid mural, and a town that funds public art at all is doing something many towns don't.

00:14:58.728 --> 00:15:03.632
<v Casey>And what matters is how much of the $5,000 the artist keeps once the paint is bought.

00:15:04.272 --> 00:15:10.032
<v Riley>Then that's the next thing to ask the sponsors. It's a question with a simple answer, and nobody has to guess.

00:15:10.560 --> 00:15:25.688
<v Casey>On to the unveilings, since that's where the town actually saw the work. Both artists presented their walls in public this fall, and the Tribune photo names Cariboo Regional District vice-chair Melynda Neufeld among the people standing there, alongside Sherry Yonkman.

00:15:26.291 --> 00:15:34.299
<v Riley>Standing next to the work, on the wall itself. A plaque goes up with nobody there. These events were better than that, and they count.

00:15:34.811 --> 00:15:46.859
<v Casey>Better, yes. A daytime unveiling reaches whoever is on Third Avenue at that hour, though. The wall faces the whole town for years. The people who saw it unveiled are a slice of that, and the photo shows who stood in the line-up.

00:15:47.519 --> 00:16:03.751
<v Riley>There's one audience the Cariboo Reflections project was built for from the start. The idea in 2019 was to replace a drab alley view for the residents of AgeCare Cariboo Place. Those neighbours see that wall every single day, whether or not they made the unveiling.

00:16:04.338 --> 00:16:08.810
<v Casey>Then they're the ones it should answer to. Did anyone ask them what they wanted to look at?

00:16:09.076 --> 00:16:21.292
<v Riley>That's a good one for the organizers. And the fix for reach generally is cheap. Put the artist's statement on the wall beside the mural, so the person who walks by in March can read what it was meant to be.

00:16:21.604 --> 00:16:23.988
<v Casey>Cheap to write. Who keeps it readable in year five?

00:16:24.088 --> 00:16:33.344
<v Casey>That's where I'd start, actually. Who repaints in year five, when the paint on an exterior wall starts to fade or peel? Upkeep is the line nobody puts in a press release.

00:16:33.669 --> 00:16:35.189
<v Riley>The wall's owner has the ladder.

00:16:35.711 --> 00:16:47.127
<v Casey>The ladder isn't the plan. Somebody has to decide the repair is worth doing and then pay for it. Is that the restaurant, the regional district, or the grant program that's already moved on to next year's round?

00:16:47.464 --> 00:16:55.560
<v Riley>I can't answer that from a press photo, and it's the gap I'd most like filled. The painters are known. Who maintains the paint isn't.

00:16:55.841 --> 00:17:00.073
<v Casey>And that question decides whether the mural is still there in 2031.

00:17:00.739 --> 00:17:11.075
<v Riley>Mulberry has the same clock problem, with a longer runway. The question there is who gets back in the room when the doors open. The question here is who keeps the paint on the wall.

00:17:11.408 --> 00:17:14.752
<v Casey>Same question, really. Who keeps the room after the ribbon-cutting?

00:17:15.076 --> 00:17:19.428
<v Riley>A public wall should have a public answer, and both cases still lack one.

00:17:19.528 --> 00:17:38.872
<v Riley>Bearden gives me another way to think about the series. That documentary jumps across his life and rhymes themes, like his trains. Put a mural series on that model and the panels stop being a timeline. They start to rhyme. A logging truck in panel one, a Chilcotin landscape in panel three: same country, different hours.

00:17:39.512 --> 00:17:49.344
<v Casey>A series organized by theme still has an editor. Somebody decides which rhyme counts and which panel goes next to which. That's still a frame, just harder to see from the sidewalk.

00:17:49.881 --> 00:17:59.041
<v Riley>Sure. But a frame that rhymes is still a conversation between painters. A newcomer doesn't have to match the first two panels. She has to speak to them.

00:17:59.638 --> 00:18:08.625
<v Casey>Speaking to them means she has to answer them. And the name is a brief in itself. "Reflections" tells a painter what to reflect on, whether or not anyone wrote that down.

00:18:08.625 --> 00:18:20.417
<v Riley>Ha. A name can be a brief, sure. It's also a shared starting point, and that's what a series is for. Without it, you get six walls with six unrelated pieces, and nobody can tell what the town is saying.

00:18:20.983 --> 00:18:32.559
<v Casey>Or you get one story, with the person who started it still holding the pen. Continuity is also pressure. Jorgensen's panel now sits in a sequence, and Lyons will paint into one with three panels already up.

00:18:33.077 --> 00:18:48.341
<v Riley>And an editor is a role somebody can be named for. That's the difference between a constraint you can see and one you can't. This one is being worked on in the open: a grant, two unveilings, a named building, named painters for four of six panels.

00:18:48.955 --> 00:18:58.907
<v Casey>Being seen is a fine start. Those unveilings beat a plaque with nobody in front of it. The rules for the series still aren't public, and a photo doesn't tell me who makes the next call.

00:18:59.434 --> 00:19:09.218
<v Riley>Then publish the rules. Panel count, how a new panel gets chosen, who signs off on each addition. That's a small, checkable step, and it costs the program nothing structural.

00:19:09.493 --> 00:19:13.701
<v Casey>It's cheap to publish. Whether anyone keeps it updated is the expensive part.

00:19:14.326 --> 00:19:19.550
<v Riley>Maybe. I'd still rather have a rulebook that can be wrong than a wall nobody can explain.

00:19:19.826 --> 00:19:21.562
<v Casey>And I'd rather know who keeps the rulebook.

00:19:21.662 --> 00:19:39.598
<v Casey>So on the Williams Lake walls: the painters did the work, and the unveilings put it in front of people. Learning that Davis, Gosling-Davis and Lyons are all part of one planned run moves me. It's more of a shared project than I assumed. What's still unproven is who answers for what the series says, and who pays to keep it on the wall.

00:19:40.282 --> 00:19:51.490
<v Riley>And I'm landing on what's already working. Two walls, two paid painters, a six-panel plan with a reason behind it, built for the people looking out from AgeCare. A town can build on that.

00:19:51.780 --> 00:19:54.172
<v Casey>It's a start, and nobody should call it settled.

00:19:54.720 --> 00:20:14.152
<v Riley>So who answers for how the whole series reads when it sits on a public body's wall? The painter, the building, or whoever organizes it? We don't have that name for Cariboo Reflections yet. If you do, or you've walked past Jorgensen's Chilcotin panel and have thoughts on it, write to feedback at cariboo signals dot c-a.

00:20:14.831 --> 00:20:22.047
<v Casey>Ask before the fourth panel goes up. And go look at the wall on your next trip downtown. That's it for this Monday. Have a good week.

00:20:22.647 --> 00:20:41.103
